---
name: Write Core Unit Test
description: Generate Jest tests for a file in the core engine layer
---

Write Jest tests for the code provided.

- Jest `^29` (core pins `^29.7.0`).
- TypeScript only. If the file under test is not TypeScript, warn and stop.
- ESM imports everywhere — never `require`.
- Import from a relative path the way the existing suites do, e.g.
  `import { testIde } from "../test/fixtures";` — depth depends on where the test
  file sits. There is no `@core` path alias.
- The test goes in an adjacent file named `<same-basename>.test.ts`. Files named
  `*.vitest.ts` belong to core's separate Vitest run; do not convert them here.
- Prefer double quotes (backticks when the string needs them).
- Aim for full coverage of the behavior that changed; split into several tests
  for clarity rather than one long one.

## What this code is

`core/` is the engine layer that the CLI drives (one engine, four surfaces). It
is no longer the backend of an editor extension. Two things still need a stand-in
and the fixtures provide them:

- workspace access, through `testIde`
- the global directory, i.e. what `~/.tnt` is for a real run, through
  `testConfigHandler` (and `ideSettingsPromise` for `IdeSettings` needs)
- models, through `testLLM` — set `testLLM.completion` to the answer you want

## Jest setup that already exists

- `core/test/jest.global-setup.ts` — creates the temporary global directory that
  persisted data lands in.
- `core/test/jest.setup-after-env.js` — exposes the node and Jest globals.
- `core/test/testDir.ts` — `setUpTestDir` / `tearDownTestDir`, called explicitly
  in any test that touches workspace files.
- `core/test/fixtures.ts` — the fixtures above.

Types and helpers come from `core/index.d.ts` and the module's own exports. If
something you need is missing, warn and write no test rather than inventing it.

## Rules

- Do not test files under `core/test`; they are helpers for testing the rest.
- Do not mock the fixtures above beyond `jest.spyOn`. Do mock third-party modules
  when sensible, and prefer generating real files and real data over a mock.
- A pure mock is for a network response, an error that is hard to reproduce, or
  to keep a test from running long.
- Do not add a banner comment to the generated file. No `// Generated by ...`.
